smartclockmon (0.6.1-1) unstable; urgency=low

  * The browser view stacks any number of series on one time axis, and
    defaults to the four the oscillator is read from: 1 PPS TI, EFC,
    internal temperature and oven current.  Over a day those four tell
    the story between them -- temperature climbing and falling, EFC
    following it almost shape for shape, and oven current mirrored
    against both.
  * The plots share their time axis by construction rather than by
    appearance: one request buckets every column in the same pass, so
    their x values are the same values.  Separate requests would each
    derive their own bucket boundaries from their own end time and land
    on a different grid -- charts that look aligned and are not.  The
    cursor moves across the stack together and a drag on any plot zooms
    all of them.
  * The two-series overlay is replaced by that rather than removed:
    tick both and they stack.
  * The sky plot's grid was drawn in the colour used for 1px panel
    borders, far too dim for lines a bearing is read off.  The rings
    and the cardinal cross are legible now, with the horizon stronger
    than the rings inside it.
  * Fixed: short plots got too few y-axis labels.  1 PPS TI over a day
    spans about -70 to +120 ns, and the axis read 100, 0 and nothing,
    leaving a third of the plot unlabelled below the zero line.
  * Fixed: the history endpoint never URL-decoded its query values.
    Harmless while the only parameter was one column name with nothing
    to encode; a list broke it at once, since a browser writes the
    separator as %2C.
  * One value readout for the whole stack rather than a legend on the
    bottom plot only: with the cursor synced there is one instant being
    pointed at, so hovering any plot shows the time once and a value
    for every series.
  * Y axes begin and end on a labelled gridline.  uPlot scales to the
    data and puts round ticks inside it, so the top and bottom lines
    fell short by a different amount on every plot; the scale is now
    snapped to the tick step.

 -- Christopher Hoover <ch@murgatroid.com>  Mon, 21 Sep 2026 22:00:39 -0700

smartclockmon (0.6.0-1) unstable; urgency=low

  * The daemon adopts a receiver on every connection rather than once
    per unit.  A connection is the boundary across which nothing is
    known -- the unit may have been power cycled, swapped, or
    reconfigured while the link was down -- so the error queue is
    drained, the transition filters re-read and the diagnostic log copy
    resumed from the database each time one is made.
  * It watches the receiver's alarm without taking it.  Reading an
    event register clears it, which clears the alarm condition register
    that summarises it, which puts out the front-panel Alarm LED and
    sets the BITE output inactive.  So no event register is read.  The
    daemon polls *STB? instead, which reports the same latched state in
    real time and changes nothing.  The lamp stays lit until you clear
    it at the receiver.
  * Nothing writes to the receiver.  No *CLS, no transition filters
    set, no alarm driven or cleared.  The one exception is opt-in and
    described below.
  * The receiver stepping its own clock to match the satellites is now
    visible.  It invalidates every interval measurement taken across
    the step and appears in no condition register; it shows as CLOCK
    STEPPED in the monitor's header, a pill in the browser's status
    strip, and smartclock_time_reset.
  * What the receiver records about itself is shown in the monitor, on
    `l`, and in the browser: its diagnostic log, alarm transitions, and
    its error queue.  The log is ordered by the receiver's own clock,
    since its entry numbers restart whenever it is cleared and the
    order it was copied in is reverse chronology.
  * New: --adopt-log erases the receiver's diagnostic log once every
    entry is held here and the receiver says it is nearly full.  Off by
    default.  The log holds 222 entries and then stops recording, so a
    unit left alone has usually stopped logging long ago -- the
    development receiver had recorded nothing since March 2025.  The
    entry count is sent with the command, so the receiver refuses if
    one arrived between the copy and the clear.
  * The transition filters are recorded beside the alarm, because
    without them it cannot be read back.  This receiver latches faults
    appearing and never clearing, so a missing clear means only that
    nobody enabled that transition.
  * Readers tolerate a log older than themselves.  The monitor and the
    browser view can be upgraded before the daemon is restarted, or
    pointed at an archived file; a table that does not exist yet reads
    as empty rather than failing the request.
  * Upgrade note: the log moves to schema 6, migrating in place.  An
    earlier smartclockd refuses a migrated log and exits 2, so a
    downgrade leaves the service stopped with the data intact.
    Recovery is forward, not back.

 -- Christopher Hoover <ch@murgatroid.com>  Mon, 21 Sep 2026 20:39:06 -0700

smartclockmon (0.5.0-1) unstable; urgency=low

  * The daemon now records what the receiver keeps about itself.  Its
    error queue is drained and written down, where before nothing read
    an error the receiver raised unprompted -- and reading an entry is
    what removes it, so a queue left alone discards what it holds.  Its
    diagnostic log is copied out entry by entry; the count was recorded
    before, which said that things had happened and never what.
  * The operation, holdover and powerup condition registers are polled
    alongside the hardware one.  Their event registers are deliberately
    left alone: reading one clears it, which would take the latched
    transition from anything else watching and retract the receiver's
    own alarm.
  * On the development unit this immediately reported a full diagnostic
    log -- 222 entries, the maximum, with a newest entry from March 2025
    -- so that receiver has silently recorded nothing for eighteen
    months.  Watch for smartclock_log_almost_full.
  * The learned oscillator temperature coefficient is polled and
    recorded.  Units are undocumented; the trend is the point.
  * Every row says which receiver it came from.  A receiver table holds
    one row per unit, keyed on the serial alone, and the snapshots,
    errors, diagnostic log entries and audit trail all carry its id.
    Before this a log file did not record what it was a log of, and a
    bench where units are swapped accumulated two oscillators' history
    with no way to tell the rows apart -- which reads as one oscillator
    with a step in it.  Rows written before this release keep a null
    receiver rather than being attributed to whatever is attached now.
  * The receiver's own UTC is polled, recorded and displayed.  The
    command existed and was called from nowhere.
  * The exporter gains smartclock_oven_tempco and the decoded condition
    bits: locked, reference_valid, position_hold, log_almost_full,
    oven_warm, date_time_valid, holdover_recovering and
    holdover_exceeding_threshold.
  * Upgrade note: the log moves to schema 4, migrating in place.  An
    earlier smartclockd refuses a migrated log and exits 2, which
    RestartPreventExitStatus= stops rather than retries, so a downgrade
    after upgrading leaves the service stopped with the data intact.
    Recovery is forward, not back.

 -- Christopher Hoover <ch@murgatroid.com>  Mon, 21 Sep 2026 09:26:43 -0700

